//
// Phase D2 block artifact cache: host-only checks on what the gate will reuse.
//
// The block gate caches its four ELFs so that the clean run and the
// fault-injected run of the same shape do not compile the layer twice. That
// saving is only sound if a cached ELF is reused when it is the binary the
// current source would produce and never otherwise -- and an artifact name,
// which carries the shape and nothing else, cannot answer that. The tiles and
// the method come from kernel_registry and the IR comes from builders this
// study edits, so four binaries whose names match the shape can still be four
// binaries nobody would build today. Running those while the results artifact
// records the freshly resolved specs is a PASS for an implementation that was
// never on the device, which is the failure this pins shut.
//
// builders/block_cache.py answers it with a fingerprint over the built MLIR, the
// resolved configuration, every device kernel source and the backend kwargs.
// The checks below hold both directions of that rule: an unchanged
// configuration reuses all four, and each thing that can change an ELF without
// changing its name forces a recompile.
//
// No NPU, no XRT and no compilation: KernelCache and the external-object builds
// are both stubbed, so this sits alongside the compile-only kernel gate and the
// Phase B seam tests and requires neither `ryzen_ai_npu2` nor `peano`. The
// hardware half of D2 is run_npu2_block_peano.lit.
//
// The test file carries no framework dependency -- pytest is not installed in
// the sandbox venv -- so it runs as a plain script and prints a pass count. The
// count is named rather than matched loosely, so a test that stops being
// collected fails here instead of passing quietly.
//
// RUN: python3 %S/builders/test_block_cache.py | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: block cache tests: 9/9 passed
